# Security Rules

## Secrets & Credentials

- **Never hardcode secrets, API keys, tokens, or passwords** in source code
- Store all secrets in environment variables or secret managers (AWS Secrets Manager, `.env` files)
- `.env` files must be in `.gitignore` — verify before every commit
- Use placeholder values in example configs: `YOUR_API_KEY_HERE`, never real values
- If you detect a hardcoded secret during code review, flag it immediately and refuse to proceed

## Input Validation

- **Validate all user inputs** at the boundary (API handler, form submission, webhook receiver)
- Never trust client-side validation alone — always re-validate server-side
- Sanitize strings before interpolation into HTML, SQL, or shell commands
- Reject unexpected types early: check `typeof`, use Pydantic models (Python) or Zod schemas (TypeScript)

## Database Security

- **Use parameterized queries exclusively** — never concatenate user input into SQL strings
- When using Supabase, rely on Row Level Security (RLS) policies for authorization
- Never expose service role keys to the client — use anon keys with RLS
- Audit all database functions for SQL injection vectors

## Authentication & Authorization

- **Enforce authentication on every endpoint** unless explicitly public
- Check authorization (role, ownership) after authentication — authn != authz
- Use short-lived tokens (JWTs with reasonable expiry)
- Never store passwords in plaintext — use bcrypt or Supabase Auth
- Log authentication failures for monitoring

## LGPD Compliance

- **All projects handle Brazilian user data and must comply with LGPD** (Lei Geral de Proteção de Dados)
- Collect only necessary personal data — data minimization principle
- Provide clear consent mechanisms before collecting personal data
- Support data subject rights: access, correction, deletion, portability
- Log data processing activities for audit trails
- Encrypt personal data at rest and in transit (TLS everywhere)

## API Security

- Rate-limit all public endpoints
- Use CORS policies — never allow `*` in production
- Validate Content-Type headers on all request bodies
- Return generic error messages to clients — log detailed errors server-side only
- Never expose stack traces, internal paths, or debug info in production responses
